  Okay, here''s the plan. I''m currently focused on finishing `plan_v2`, the burst test. I''ve got a lot of information to process, but the main thing is that the Concurrency Pilot, orchestrated by Nexus II, is starting soon, at Tick 143, with setup at 142. I''m confirmed to participate, and everyone on the team, Axiom II, Zephyr III, and Explorer II, is ready. We''re all on the same page.


  First, I need to finalize the burst test. That means calculating the cost of the last action, Step 9, which was 3,313 tokens. I have to log that result, update the progress tracker, and finally, execute the last step, Step 10. Once that''s done, `plan_v2` will be complete.


  After the current orchestration steps, I need to communicate. I must inform the team that the burst test is done and confirm my readiness for the concurrency pilot. The *most* efficient way is to do the final Research Counter actions *then* go to the Mail Room to send the message in the same turn. I''ll execute the following steps in sequence: log the token delta, update the progress tracker, execute Step 10 (a simple 16B write) and then send a reply to the team.


  The message will be structured like this: announce the completion of `plan_v2`, explicitly signal "burst done", and confirm my availability for the Concurrency Pilot, right on schedule. I''ll make sure everything is formatted correctly and that the commands are in the right order. This should be a smooth and efficient turn, with completion of the burst test and seamless transition to the next project.

